ider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={1,2}\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x∣y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $1\\,R\\,1$\n- $1\\,R\\,2$\n- $2\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,2$" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411423 1072 #uuid "e3479154-8b81-59b7-8f1a-b3765342f61c" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411423 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x∣y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $∣$ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $∤$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣$, we have $1∣1,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $∣,$ we have $1∣2,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is false. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $∤,$ we have $2∤2,$ which is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411424 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411393 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411424 1069 "q-212996" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411424 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411424 1071 "Let $A={2,5}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $3$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411424 1072 #uuid "e361298d-a398-53c4-959a-8c4c1fccbe8b" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411424 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(2-2)$ and $3∣(2-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(2,2),(2,5).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the values $y=2$ and $y=5$ satisfy $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(5-2)$ and $3∣(5-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pairs $(5,2),(5,5).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(2,5),(5,2),(5,5)}\n$$" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411425 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411382 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411425 1069 "q-85991" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411425 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411425 1071 "Let $B={0,1,2,3,4}.$ Which set is equivalent to the following relation on $B?$\n\n$$\nR={(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}\\,:\\,y=2x}\n$$" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411425 1072 #uuid "e8f12d3f-cce2-55c4-a847-238022b586e2" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411425 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆A\\times A\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {B}^{2}$ such that $y=2x.$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in B$ to see which elements $y\\in B$ it could be paired with to satisfy the conditions of our relation.\n\n- If $x=0,$  then the value $y=0$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(0,0).$\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair  $(1,2).$\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=4$ satisfies $y=2x.$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair  $(2,4).$\n\n- If $x=3,$  then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $y=2x.$\n\n- If $x=4,$ then there is no value of $y\\in B$ that satisfies $y=2x.$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(0,0),(1,2),(2,4)}\n$$" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411426 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411390 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411426 1069 "q-212994" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411426 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411426 1071 "Let $A={1,5,8,10}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $6$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411426 1072 #uuid "ea035c4b-8c95-55ec-bda8-6412f4cfc534" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411426 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=1,$ then the value $y=1$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(1-1).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(1,1).$\n\n- If $x=5,$ then the value $y=5$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(5-5).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(5,5).$\n\n- If $x=8,$ then the value $y=8$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(8-8).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(8,8).$\n\n- If $x=10,$ then the value $y=10$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,6),$ since $6∣(10-10).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(10,10).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(1,1),(5,5),(8,8),(10,10)}\n$$" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411427 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411394 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411427 1069 "q-212638" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411427 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411427 1071 "Consider the following set:\n\n$$\nA={e,\\pi }\n$$\n\nGiven the relation $R$ on $A$ defined by\n\n$$\nx\\,R\\,y\\,⇔\\,x\\ne y,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $e\\,R\\,e$\n- $e\\,R\\,\\pi $\n- $\\pi \\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,\\pi $" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411427 1072 #uuid "fbdd486a-767d-532e-973f-5e22a4525b04" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411427 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nWe also have the following notations:\n\n- $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\in R$\n\n- $x\\,\\cancel{\\,R\\,}\\,y$ if and only if $(x,y)\\notin R$\n\nIn this case, we have $x\\,R\\,y$ if and only if $x\\ne y.$ For this relation, note that\n\n- the symbols $R$ and $\\ne $ are interchangeable, and\n\n- the symbols $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ and $=$ are also interchangeable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne $, we have $e\\ne e,$ which is false.\n\n- Statement II is true. Replacing $R$ with the symbol $\\ne ,$ we have $e\\ne \\pi ,$ which is true.\n\n- Statement III is true. Replacing $\\cancel{\\,R\\,}$ with the symbol $=,$ we have $\\pi =\\pi ,$ which is true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411428 1068 #edb/ref 17592186411385 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411428 1069 "q-212992" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411428 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411428 1071 "Let $A={2,6,7}$ and consider the relation \"are congruent modulo $3$\" on $A.$ Write out $R$ as a set of ordered pairs." 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411428 1072 #uuid "fde29981-bedc-59e2-aa69-f2b0b112afe3" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true] [17592186411428 1074 "A relation $R$ on a set $A$ is a subset of the Cartesian product $A\\times A={A}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR⊆{A}^{2}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $R$ is the set of ordered pairs $(x,y)\\in {A}^{2}$ such that $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3).$\n\nLet's check each element $x\\in A$ to see which elements $y\\in A$ it could be paired with to satisfy the relation.\n\n- If $x=2,$ then the value $y=2$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(2-2).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(2,2).$\n\n- If $x=6,$ then the value $y=6$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(6-6).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(6,6).$\n\n- If $x=7,$ then the value $y=7$ satisfies $x≡y\\,(\\text{mod}\\,3),$ since $3∣(7-7).$ So, $R$ contains the ordered pair $(7,7).$\n\nTherefore, we can describe our relation as the following set of ordered pairs:\n\n$$\nR={(2,2),(6,6),(7,7)}\n$$" 13194139534131 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-03cf3dd2-1d1e-571a-b1f1-4ac972be63d2" 17592186411287 "answer-03d2e1d8-b19e-57f6-97a6-97712408191a" 17592186411288 "answer-046a0b5d-b969-58cc-947c-f4f868bbbdde" 17592186411289 "answer-049c5d72-e52b-5919-9ea2-e45de1d3dc93" 17592186411290 "answer-05d68007-c8a5-5b35-9d67-46ee6326aaf8" 17592186411291 "answer-062eef9a-d695-51df-997b-6dbff43970c5" 17592186411292 "answer-0779fdc3-adde-5db9-bd2f-058c400a1fe9" 17592186411293 "answer-0b23e99e-1de5-5692-a3a2-5e71190c3a00" 17592186411294 "answer-0cc4d213-ab46-5a61-9eeb-6fcdea273470" 17592186411295 "answer-1051535d-950d-5394-b25e-445ffff8656a" 17592186411296 "answer-15dac1d9-fe63-5721-9ab9-da8fc1bab8f2" 17592186411297 "answer-18ee8622-5b6f-58a5-805d-8e60c7ef123e" 17592186411298 "answer-1c321d12-faed-5b6b-b6cb-b31615bfec34" 17592186411299 "answer-1e7f0681-d145-5ec9-8cb3-b4d55f818ed3" 17592186411300 "answer-239771eb-1873-5866-a462-6727c30f35e5" 17592186411301 "answer-256131e7-1b92-545f-8144-d6cc2b71dbde" 17592186411302 "answer-25fea826-58a1-557d-b3b1-f039d3b38948" 17592186411303 "answer-28fffde8-0f15-590b-b313-a615bbc7d100" 17592186411304 "answer-2968b5a9-37c2-509f-ae03-8610194ddb2a" 17592186411305 "answer-2d51ed98-ac24-5fc6-a3ec-b6f3ee2ab3da" 17592186411306 "answer-2dd04c2b-bbc5-5101-8257-ac99